    {
      "filename": "Katavothra-Feneos ponor karst-depression Peloponnese Feneos mit katavothre.jpg",
      "title": "File:Katavothra-Feneos ponor karst-depression Peloponnese Feneos mit katavothre.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Katavothra-Feneos_ponor_karst-depression_Peloponnese_Feneos_mit_katavothre.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Katavothra-Feneos_ponor_karst-depression_Peloponnese_Feneos_mit_katavothre.jpg",
      "width": 1547,
      "height": 1800,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "katsenis",
      "description": "One of 3 Ponors in the southern part of the polje Feneos, Corinthia, Peloponnese.\nWhen precipitation  of winters is exceptionally rich, parts of the polje become flooded even nowadays; until the second half of the 19th century there was even an almost permanent lake. Marks of the watertable at the bottom of the slopes are still visible. Construction of a system of draining ditches reduced floods.\n\nBy hydrogeological tracertests in 1984 subsurface drainage through the surrounding limestone mountains emerged in a karst spring of river Ladonas, 15 km south east.",
      "dateCreated": "2012",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "pheneus-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sw8p",
      "smallPath": "_m/s/q/x/z/pheneus-wp/hero-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Feneos-Polje Pheneos-limni Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "title": "File:Feneos-Polje Pheneos-limni Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Feneos-Polje_Pheneos-limni_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feneos-Polje_Pheneos-limni_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1194,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en",
      "artist": "ulrichstill",
      "description": "Polje Fenoes, a plateau in 720 m, Prefecture Corinthia. The plateau has no natural surface water outlet, which is a relatively frequent geological phenomenon in Karstbasins (island Peloponnese in Greece).\nTwo small streams, Olvios and Doxa  (Όλβιος, Δόξας) empty into the polje. When precipitation  of winters is exceptionally rich, the polje is partly flooded, a temporary lake may result even nowadays, when the capacity of the three ponors is insufficient or the ponors, or their subsurface waterways, are  blocked.\n\nUp to the beginning of the twenties century the polje rather was a lake than a ground for agriculture.",
      "dateCreated": "15th May, 2006, time: 18:40",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "corinthia-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sw8p",
      "smallPath": "_m/s/w/8/p/corinthia-wp/feneos-polje-pheneos-limni-pel-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Fenos-Lake+Polje Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "title": "File:Fenos-Lake+Polje Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Fenos-Lake%2BPolje_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fenos-Lake%2BPolje_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1197,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "vlassis",
      "description": "Polje Fenoes, a plateau in 720 m, Prefecture Corinthia. The plateau has no natural surface water outlet, which is a relatively frequent geological phenomenon in Karstbasins (island Peloponnese in Greece).\nTwo small streams, Olvios and Doxa  (Όλβιος, Δόξας) empty into the polje. When precipitation of winters is exceptionally large, the soil of the polje is partly wet. A temporary lake may result even nowadays, when the capacity of the three ponors is insufficient or the ponors, or their subsurface waterways, are  blocked.",
